  "teaching": {
    "task": "# Year 12 Physics M5 V14: Banked roads. How does a banked road reduce reliance on friction? (design)",
    "causalExplanation": "On a flat road, the normal force points straight up, so it can't help with the turn. But the normal force is always at right angles to the road. Tilt the road by an angle theta, and the normal force tilts by the same angle from the vertical. Now it has two parts. Its vertical part, N cosine theta, holds the car up. Its horizontal part, N sine theta, points towards the centre of the bend. That inward part is something a flat road just can't give. Here's an important choice. The car goes round a horizontal circle, so its acceleration is horizontal, towards the centre. So it's simplest to split the forces horizontally and vertically, rather than along the slope. On ice, only two forces act, the weight and the normal force. Vertically there's no acceleration, so N cosine theta equals m g. Horizontally, N sine theta is the whole net force, so N sine theta equals m v squared over r. It's the same pattern as the conical pendulum, with the road's push in place of the string's pull. Divide one equation by the other, and N and m both cancel. Tan theta equals v squared over r g, exactly as for the pendulum. So there's one speed where the bank does the whole job by itself. It's called the design speed, and v equals the square root of r g tan theta. At that speed, no friction is needed at all. The mass cancelled too, so it's the same design speed for a truck or a small car.",
